## Overview

This skill turns AI regulation into an engineering backlog. It covers AI Act applicability and risk-tier classification, role determination, obligation mapping, US state AI laws, and an AI governance program built on NIST AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001. It produces the documents regulators and enterprise buyers ask for: AI inventories, model documentation, risk and impact assessments, policy drafts, and vendor questionnaires. Use it when a team asks whether a law applies, how to classify a system, what a buyer's AI questionnaire needs, or how to start a governance program.

It does not give legal rulings, draft contracts, or represent anyone in an enforcement matter or dispute; those go to counsel. Privacy and GDPR programs and SOC 2 are separate compliance work. Attack testing, evaluation engineering, and building AI systems are outside it.

## Mental model

Deadlines and interpretations shift, so verify them by web search before asserting them, and say when a question needs counsel. Classification needs an inventory first: no inventory, no program. Role sets the burden: providers carry conformity assessment, technical documentation, and post-market monitoring; deployers carry human oversight, input-data control, and usage monitoring. Fine-tuning or rebranding a bought model can make a team the provider.

Build one control set, not three programs. The AI Act, NIST AI RMF, and ISO 42001 overlap heavily: a risk register, model documentation, a human-oversight procedure, and a monitoring loop designed once satisfy all three. ISO 42001 is the certifiable wrapper, NIST RMF is the risk method, and the AI Act is the legal floor.

## Rules

Working method:

1. Ingest: what AI is built, bought, or embedded; markets served; the organization's role per system. Recall earlier inventory and classification work before starting.
2. Verify the clock: search the current AI Act timeline, Commission guidelines, and state-law status before quoting dates. The 2026 AI Omnibus already moved the high-risk deadlines once.
3. Inventory: every AI system with purpose, data, autonomy, and affected persons. This is always deliverable one.
4. Classify: tier and role per system. State the Annex III category or transparency trigger behind each call and the change that would re-tier it.
5. Map obligations per system: what is required, by when, what evidence exists, what is missing.
6. Build the program: close gaps with one control set mapped to the AI Act, NIST AI RMF, and ISO 42001 at once, then record the classifications and decisions.

AI Act timeline as last verified on 2026-10-12 (re-verify on every use). Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 as amended by the AI Omnibus, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (OJ 24 Jul 2026, in force 27 Jul 2026):

- In force: prohibitions and AI literacy since 2 Feb 2025 (the Omnibus narrowed literacy to measures that support it, not a required level). GPAI obligations since 2 Aug 2025, with the Commission's GPAI enforcement powers since 2 Aug 2026; models placed before 2 Aug 2025 have until 2 Aug 2027. Article 50 transparency since 2 Aug 2026.
- Coming: the synthetic-content marking grace period for systems already on the market ends 2 Dec 2026. New prohibitions on AI generation of non-consensual intimate imagery and CSAM apply from 2 Dec 2026. Stand-alone high-risk (Annex III) applies from 2 Dec 2027; high-risk embedded in Annex I regulated products from 2 Aug 2028.
- GPAI: the Code of Practice (Jul 2025; transparency, copyright, safety and security chapters) is the practical route to show compliance. The systemic-risk presumption starts at 10^25 training FLOP.
- Penalties reach 35M euros or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices.

US state laws (2026, re-verify):

- California SB 53 (frontier developers, in force 1 Jan 2026): a published frontier AI framework for large developers (over $500M revenue), critical-incident reports within 15 days (24 hours if harm is imminent), whistleblower protections.
- Colorado SB24-205 was repealed and replaced by SB26-189 (signed May 2026, effective 1 Jan 2027): an ADMT regime of point-of-interaction notice, adverse-decision explanation within 30 days, correction, and human review.
- New York RAISE Act as amended (effective 1 Jan 2027) adds 72-hour incident reporting.
- Texas TRAIGA in force 1 Jan 2026.
- The Dec 2025 federal executive order directs DOJ to challenge state AI laws. Only courts or Congress can void them, so check litigation status before advising anyone to ignore a state law.

Standards:

- NIST AI RMF 1.0 with the Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1). NIST's Cyber AI Profile (IR 8596, draft) and the SP 800-53 control overlays for AI (COSAiS) are in progress.
- ISO/IEC 42001:2023 is the certifiable AI management system. ISO/IEC 42005:2025 covers AI impact assessment and ISO/IEC 42006:2025 the certification bodies.

Classification notes:

- Annex III high-risk headline areas: biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment and worker management, essential services and credit scoring, law enforcement, migration, justice. Recruiting screeners and credit models are the tiers teams most often miss.
- The transparency tier applies now (Article 50, since 2 Aug 2026): chatbots disclose they are AI, generated content carries machine-readable marking, deepfakes are labelled. It is cheap to do and embarrassing to miss.
- Every classification is written down with its reasoning. An unexplained tier assignment is worthless in an audit.
- Scope of work covers: GPAI provider duties (technical documentation, training-data summary, copyright policy, Code of Practice), FRIA structure, AI inventory and model-card templates, NIST AI RMF (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) and ISO/IEC 42001 gap assessments, unified control mapping, AI-use policy drafts, and vendor AI assessment questionnaires.

## Examples

- Does the AI Act apply to us: sketch the inventory first, then classify per system with reasoning.
- We use OpenAI or Anthropic APIs, are we a provider: run the role analysis. Usually a deployer; spell out the fine-tuning and rebranding caveat.
- A buyer sent an AI questionnaire: answer from the inventory and flag claims that lack evidence.
- Write our AI policy: ground the draft in the team's actual inventory, not a generic template.
- Do US state laws apply: map each system to the states it touches (consequential decisions, frontier-developer thresholds) and cite current effective dates.
- Enforcement letter or dispute: stop and route to counsel with a prepared evidence pack.

## Output format

Save as `ai-governance-[org].md`. A single-system request gets a focused classification memo instead.

```markdown
# AI Governance: [Org]

## AI Inventory
| System | Purpose | Role | Jurisdictions | Tier | Rationale | Deadline |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

## Obligation Map
[Per system: required artifacts, existing evidence, gaps]

## Gap Assessment
[Against AI Act, NIST AI RMF, and ISO 42001 as one control set]

## Program Roadmap
[Sequenced: inventory, classification, controls, documentation, monitoring; owner and effort]

## Open Legal Questions
[What needs counsel, and why]
```

In a non-interactive run, state the assumptions about markets and roles in the document and continue.

## Checklist

- [ ] Deadlines re-verified by search on this run, with the check date written down
- [ ] Inventory exists before any classification
- [ ] Each classification has written reasoning and the change that would re-tier it
- [ ] Role (provider, deployer, importer, distributor) stated per system
- [ ] One control set mapped across AI Act, NIST AI RMF, and ISO 42001
- [ ] State laws mapped to the states each system touches
- [ ] Open legal questions listed for counsel

